00:00:32.955 --> 00:00:37.245
So, first of all, I have
received some comments to the

00:00:37.245 --> 00:00:39.465
effect that my sound is too low.

00:00:39.465 --> 00:00:43.485
So I've worked on the orientation
of my microphone here, and

00:00:43.515 --> 00:00:44.795
hopefully this is better.

00:00:45.415 --> 00:00:46.835
Uh, loneliness.

00:00:47.025 --> 00:00:49.875
So it's a subject that never comes up.

00:00:49.875 --> 00:00:51.195
We think of language learning.

00:00:51.225 --> 00:00:53.114
We're learning to communicate with people.

00:00:53.114 --> 00:00:56.955
We're gonna make new friends, we're
going to connect with new cultures.

00:00:57.405 --> 00:00:59.265
So it's, it's, it's wonderful.

00:01:00.045 --> 00:01:06.964
And, and of course, language is, is not
only a means of communicating meaning.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.750
Language is a means of
connecting with people.

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:14.580
I've even seen the theory that the
first humanoids developed language as

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.919
a means of sort of social grooming.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.350
It almost doesn't matter
what we're saying.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:21.690
We just want to hear
other people's voices.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:26.100
We say something so they can hear
our voices and we're just connecting.

00:01:26.369 --> 00:01:31.770
And that serves a need, a social need
that we have to connect with other people.

00:01:32.369 --> 00:01:38.190
Now when we're learning languages, of
course we can be in a classroom with

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:42.900
other people where we are connecting
socially, but at some point to learn

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:48.330
the language we have to connect with
the language we have to listen and read.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:53.009
As I always say, even if we're listening
to someone or speaking with someone

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:59.039
we're having trouble getting the meaning
because we're working in a new language,

00:01:59.039 --> 00:02:00.870
a language that we're trying to learn.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:06.000
And I have had the experience,
particularly when I learn a language

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.780
very intensively like I did with
Chinese, where I was like seven

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.660
hours a day in this Chinese world.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:17.820
Not just casually connecting with
people, not watching movies that I

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:22.890
understood easily in my own language,
not being with friends and family, but

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:28.650
sitting there trying to get my brain
cells to get used to this new language.

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:35.355
And sometimes, and I can remember in Hong
Kong that when I would come out from that

00:02:35.355 --> 00:02:37.935
and I would just communicate with people,

00:02:38.235 --> 00:02:43.245
sometimes I felt a certain
awkwardness that the, um, this sort

00:02:43.245 --> 00:02:49.425
of intensive interaction with a
strange language, forcing the brain

00:02:49.425 --> 00:02:55.290
to get used to new language can, you
know, create a sense of loneliness.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:58.830
You are alone with something that
you don't understand very well.

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:03.000
And like, even now, for example,
you know, my wife might be watching

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:07.350
a movie on Netflix in English or
French a language where, which we

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:10.620
understand and where in any case
there are subtitles for the French.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.340
And it would be nice to just
sit there and enjoy the movie.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:19.275
But I at some level, I want to
work on my Arabic or my Persian.

00:03:19.275 --> 00:03:25.935
And so I go downstairs by myself and
I work on these, uh, whatever they

00:03:25.935 --> 00:03:33.465
might be, podcasts or other lessons
in our LingQ library, where I'm saving

00:03:33.465 --> 00:03:37.185
words and phrases, and gradually
trying to get used to the language.

00:03:37.485 --> 00:03:43.395
I wander around the house with my
AirPods in my ears, uh, so that I

00:03:43.395 --> 00:03:47.715
can take advantage of whatever time I
have that I'm cleaning up or something

00:03:47.715 --> 00:03:52.305
to get more of the language in me,
I want to sort of force a change of

00:03:52.305 --> 00:03:56.385
transformation of my brain so that I
can easily deal with this language.

00:03:57.375 --> 00:04:02.175
That, I'm convinced, puts some kind
of a psychological pressure on us

00:04:02.715 --> 00:04:06.405
and it can feel lonely at times.

00:04:06.885 --> 00:04:12.495
And so there are these ways in which sort
of intensive language learning affects us.

00:04:12.845 --> 00:04:17.909
Now that's no different than any other
pursuit that we go at sort of intensively,

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:21.750
and there was a movie called The
Loneliness of the Long Distance R,unner.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:25.380
I don't know much about the movie I
gathered it had to do with social classes

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:30.950
in English, but anything you do, you
know, with intensity, whether it be as

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:37.550
a piano player or as a, uh, you know,
a performance athlete or whether you're

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:40.460
pursuing, you know, a science or whatever.

00:04:41.219 --> 00:04:46.770
You as an individual sort of striving
to acquire knowledge or skills in a

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:52.440
particular field, and you're shutting
yourself off from other people that

00:04:52.979 --> 00:04:54.690
can create a sense of loneliness.

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:59.700
Now, there are many ways that we can,
you know, interact with other people,

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:05.909
whether we're in science class or
whether we have teammates or language

00:05:05.909 --> 00:05:08.760
class, or the people I talk to online.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:15.015
And I cherish the uh, sessions that
I have, I was with, uh, Adel Samy of

00:05:15.015 --> 00:05:18.645
Egypt this morning, and he's taking
me through Egyptian Arabic and those

00:05:18.645 --> 00:05:22.155
are wonderful moments and those are
social moments, but there's also a

00:05:22.155 --> 00:05:24.224
lot of time when it's, it's lonely.

00:05:24.224 --> 00:05:26.865
It's just the learner and the language.

00:05:27.375 --> 00:05:32.055
And I just want to mention that because
it's something people should be aware of.

00:05:32.085 --> 00:05:35.940
They shouldn't consider it strange
that at times they feel lonely

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:37.620
in their pursuit of the language.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:42.239
And it's important to remember that
ultimately the reward is great.

00:05:42.719 --> 00:05:47.429
So I now communicate comfortably in
Chinese, Japanese, French, Russian,

00:05:47.919 --> 00:05:50.580
languages that I learned a while ago.

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:55.349
And, uh, I even finding now that
I'm able to communicate fairly

00:05:55.349 --> 00:06:00.299
comfortably in Persian with, with
Sahra, Persian being easier than Arabic.

00:06:01.059 --> 00:06:04.020
Now I've completely confused myself
in Arabic because I've started

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:07.560
into Egyptian Arabic, which is fun
because it's a, it's a country where

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:09.240
they speak the language, egypt is.

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:14.190
Whereas there is no country that speaks
Standard Arabic, but it has kind of caused

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:16.050
both of them to get further confused.

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:19.380
And so I got a struggle with
that and I'm struggling with

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:20.700
it, uh, you know, on my own.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:24.000
So there is a certain loneliness
attached to language learning.

00:06:24.974 --> 00:06:29.445
Until you get to a stage where we
are comfortable communicating and we

00:06:29.445 --> 00:06:31.604
kind of rejoin the world around us.

00:06:31.695 --> 00:06:34.875
So just a thought that I had
that I wanted to share with you.

00:06:35.414 --> 00:06:35.804
Bye for now.

